2.3  CPU caches: why memory layout matters

TL;DR
  - RAM is about 100 times slower than the CPU's own registers. Small, fast
    memories in the chip keep recent data close: L1 (tens of KiB per core, a few
    cycles), L2 (MiB, about a dozen cycles), L3 (shared, tens of cycles).
  - Memory moves in cache lines, 64 bytes on most CPUs: reading one byte brings
    its neighbours along.
  - So data read in order, packed together, is fast; data scattered in memory
    (linked lists, pointers to pointers) is slow.

      lscpu --caches
      perf stat -e cache-misses,cache-references ./program

On your machine
  L1 data   4 × 48.0 KiB, each shared by 2 CPUs
  L1 code   4 × 32.0 KiB, each shared by 2 CPUs
  L2        4 × 1.2 MiB, each shared by 2 CPUs
  L3        12.0 MiB, shared by all 8 CPUs
  Line      64 bytes: the block memory moves in

Pitfalls
  - Going through a large 2D array column by column instead of row by row can be
    several times slower: each access lands on a new line.
  - False sharing: two threads writing different variables that sit on the same
    line make that line bounce between their cores. Pad or separate hot
    per-thread data.
  - A benchmark whose data fits in cache says little about the same code on data
    ten times bigger.

Deep dive

  ▾ Who decides what stays in the cache?

    The hardware, by itself, invisibly to programs. When a cache is full, the
    least recently used line goes. The CPU also prefetches: when it sees you
    read lines in order, it fetches the next ones before you ask. That's why a
    sequential scan of memory runs close to full speed, and random access
    doesn't.

  ▾ How do cores agree on memory?

    Each core has its own L1 and L2, so two could hold different copies of the
    same line. A coherence protocol prevents it: before a core writes a line,
    every other copy is invalidated. That traffic is the hidden cost of sharing
    data between threads, and why atomic counters updated by many threads scale
    badly.

Related
  Commands  lscpu, perf
  Paths     /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cache/
  Hub       atfq cpu CPU
